# Deploying `{{.Target}}` to SLNG

Unmute's compiler wrote the files in this directory and stopped. It opens no
connection to SLNG at any point, so nothing here has been pushed, checked
against your account, or reserved. `{{.DeployCommand}}` does that part, by
handing these files to the `voiceai` CLI.

Everything in this directory is disposable. Edit the package and compile again.

| File | What it is |
|---|---|
| `agent.json` | the agent create body |
| `README.md` | this file |
| `compile-report.json` | what was compiled, and which checks were left to deployment |

The agent deploys to region **{{.Region}}**{{if eq .Region "any"}}, which lets
SLNG route each call itself{{end}}.

{{if .NeedsVault -}}
## 1. Create these Vault entries first

SLNG reads these from its own secret store. No secret value appears in any file
in this directory or on any command line below, and unmute has never seen one.

This is only what compiling your package could find written in it: a declared
`auth:` or a `{{"{{$NAME}}"}}` token in your prompt or greeting.{{if or .HostedRefs .MCPRefs}} A hosted tool or MCP
server's own credential is not on this list either way: SLNG holds it, and this
compile never asked SLNG anything.{{end}} `unmute deploy` reads your
organisation directly and checks or creates whatever the resolved tools
actually need, so a run may ask about an entry that never appears below.

You do not have to create these by hand. `unmute deploy` checks this list, and
whatever else it finds, against your organisation before it writes anything,
and offers to create what is missing: it hands the prompt to `voiceai`, which
masks what you type, so the value never passes through unmute. Create them in
the SLNG dashboard instead if you prefer. A name is {{.NameShape}}.

{{if .VaultSecrets -}}
**Secrets** — credentials a tool authenticates with.

| Vault name | Needed because |
|---|---|
{{range .VaultSecrets}}| `{{.Name}}` | {{.Where}} |
{{end}}
{{end -}}
{{if .VaultVariables -}}
**Variables** — values SLNG substitutes into text at run time, written in the
package as `{{"{{$NAME}}"}}` tokens. Nothing declares them in the package: SLNG
holds the value, so only a slng target resolves one.

| Vault name | Needed because |
|---|---|
{{range .VaultVariables}}| `{{.Name}}` | {{.Where}} |
{{end}}
{{end -}}
{{else -}}
## 1. Vault entries

This package needs none, as far as this compile can tell.{{if or .HostedRefs .MCPRefs}} A hosted tool or MCP server's own credential is invisible from
here either way: SLNG holds it, and this compile never asked SLNG anything, so
`unmute deploy` may still find one to check or create before it changes the
agent.{{else}} Nothing in it names a secret or a Vault variable token, so there is nothing to create before you push.{{end}}
{{end}}
## 2. Deploy

One command validates the package, compiles it again, and pushes it:

```bash
{{.DeployCommand}}
```

Run it from the package root, the directory above `build/`.

It reads the key from `{{.RouterKeyEnv}}`, falling back to `{{.CredentialEnv}}`
and then to whatever profile `{{.LoginCommand}}` stored:

```bash
export {{.RouterKeyEnv}}=...
```

One SLNG key serves every SLNG role, so this is the same key a generated livekit
or pipecat project reads at run time. `{{.CredentialEnv}}` is the name the push
tool itself reads, and either works.

The organisation the push resolved is printed on every run. That matters because
an exported key and a stored profile can belong to **different** organisations,
and nothing else on screen would tell you which one you just wrote to.

Deployment requires a compatible `voiceai`; this flow was verified with 0.1.18.
Use `--dry-run` to preview checked versions and attachment changes without
changing SLNG. Future session inputs remain runtime checks. The result is saved
in `deploy-report.json` beside these files.

`--run-samples` has nothing to act on here. A sample proves a tool works before
the platform publishes it, and this push creates no tool: it writes references
to tools your organisation already holds, each published before your package
named it.

**A push replaces.** A tool reference this package no longer names is detached,
and a field that differs from the live agent is overwritten. `--dry-run` names
both before anything happens.

**Which agent it replaces is decided by the name.** This package pushes
**{{.AgentName}}**: the `name:` in your `agent.yaml` joined to the `{{.Target}}`
target, never the target alone. SLNG names are unique in your organisation and a
push writes whichever agent matches, so before the first push of a new package,
check the name is free:

```bash
{{.ListCommand}}
```

Rename in `agent.yaml` and compile again if it is taken. Renaming later does not
move a deployed agent: it leaves the old one running and writes a second.

The agent is written only after its required checks pass. A real deploy may
first refresh MCP discovery or fill a Vault entry with your consent; if a later
check fails, the report names those completed changes. A dry run does neither.
Each problem names what to fix and the relevant dashboard page.

An agent id comes back. Keep it for step 3, or find it again with
`voiceai agents list`: unmute stores no id, writes no lockfile, and keeps no
local state about your account.

### Driving the push tool yourself

The standalone push command is also available:

```bash
{{.PushCommand}}
```

It takes the package root or this directory, but bypasses Unmute's published
binding checks and checked-version staging. Use `{{.DeployCommand}}` for the
checked deployment flow. Do **not** post `agent.json` with
`voiceai agents create` instead: that sends the body verbatim, and the body
carries a name in every place the API wants an identifier.

{{if .NeedsPushResolution -}}
### What the push resolves for you

`agent.json` carries a name where the API wants an id, because no compiler can
invent an id a server assigns. Each entry under `tool_refs` is written as
`{"tool": "<name>", ...}`, and the API wants `attachment_id`, `tool_id` and
`version`, all three required, with no `tool` field at all. `mcp_refs` is the
same shape.

The push step resolves those names against your organisation's tools and mints
the attachment ids. It creates none of them: every name here is a tool your
organisation already holds. Nothing here is yours to edit by hand.

**A curated capability is no exception.** A builtin such as `end_call` needs
nothing *created*, and its `tool_id` still has to be filled in, which the push
does.
{{if .Builtins}}
This package's curated references: {{range $i, $name := .Builtins}}{{if $i}}, {{end}}`{{$name}}`{{end}}.
{{end}}
{{if .HostedRefs -}}
**This target creates no tool.** Every tool the body names is one your
organisation already holds, referenced by name, and the deploy resolves each
name to its identifier and its latest published version. A name your
organisation does not have is a refusal, not something the push makes for you: a
new tool starts in the SLNG dashboard.

These are the hosted names; deployment selects and checks their published versions:

{{range .HostedRefs}}- {{.}}
{{end}}
The published version supplies each tool's description and its parameters, so
nothing about them is written here and there is no local copy to keep in step.
Your tool file names the tool and holds what you add to it: an `inject:` value,
an `announce:` sentence, a `description:` when you deliberately want different
words from the platform's.

Three things about these references this compile could not check, because they
are facts about your organisation rather than about your package: that a tool of
each name exists, that each injected argument is one of its published
parameters, and which Vault entries it needs. `unmute deploy` completes all
three before it changes the agent, and `--dry-run` reports them without
changing anything. `compile-report.json` lists them as deferred.

`unmute pull` is optional and belongs to the other two targets: it mirrors a
hosted tool's definition into your package so a `livekit` or `pipecat` build of
the same package has a tool to compile. A SLNG-only package needs no mirror.

To try one on its own, first write a JSON input file matching its published
parameters at `samples/<tool>.json`; this compiler creates no sample file.
Use the same account with `export {{.CredentialEnv}}="${{.RouterKeyEnv}}"`.

```
voiceai tool run <tool> --input samples/<tool>.json --confirm-side-effects
```

That runs the copy SLNG already has, against your real dependencies, so it
changes whatever the tool changes.

{{end -}}
